# 7. Medical Records and Clinical Data Integrity

Patient profile information and clinician-authored medical records must remain distinct.

## 7.1 Patient-Provided Information

Examples include:
- Patient-reported blood group.
- Patient-reported height and weight.
- Patient-reported allergies.
- Patient-reported medical conditions.
- Patient-reported medications.

Represent these fields as patient-provided information.

Where relevant, display an appropriate indicator such as "Reported by patient" or "Not clinically verified."

Do not automatically present self-reported information as clinically verified.

## 7.2 Clinician-Authored Medical Records

When a doctor creates a prescription, consultation note, diagnosis, or other clinical record:

- Associate the record with the correct patient.
- Record its author and relevant consultation context.
- Preserve the application's existing clinical-record structure.
- Enforce authorization on the backend.
- Maintain appropriate audit trails.
- Prevent unauthorized modification or deletion.
- Do not allow patient profile updates to overwrite clinician-authored records.

## 7.3 Role-Based Medical-Record Access

Preserve the intended separation between patient-facing and staff-facing functionality.

- Patients may access their own authorized records through supported workflows.
- Doctors may access records permitted by their role, clinic association, and applicable authorization rules.
- Receptionists should not have access to medical-record contents unless a specific, authorized workflow requires it.
- Unauthorized users must not gain access by changing patient identifiers in API requests.

Do not implement access restrictions solely by hiding frontend components.

